////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Module      : w1c32
// Description : AXI4-Lite register file (made from its HDL spec, <module>.hdlspec.json:
//               only the data that changes this code; see the register map document for
//               descriptions and versions)
//
// Generated by AXIL Studio (axilgen) from ZipCPU wb2axip rtl/easyaxil.v; the
// original header follows. Changes to easyaxil.v, all in the places easyaxil
// leaves to the user:
//   - module name, C_AXI_ADDR_WIDTH default, the IP-side ports
//   - "AXI-lite register logic": the registers of this IP instead of r0..r3;
//     word address bits [2:2] are decoded (the map repeats every 0x8 bytes),
//     unused offsets read 0 (default branch of the read case)
//   - C_AXI_DATA_WIDTH: `localparam` in the parameter list (SystemVerilog only)
//     written as `parameter` for Verilog-2005 tools. Do not override it.
//   - formal: the r0..r3 register checks are left out (faxil_slave checks kept)
// With OPT_SKIDBUFFER=1 skidbuffer.v (wb2axip) is needed.
//
// Register map
//   0x00  EV               RW
//          [31:0]   E                W1C   irq
//   0x04  P                WO
//          [31:0]   GO               PULSE
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//
// Filename:	rtl/easyaxil.v
// {{{
// Project:	WB2AXIPSP: bus bridges and other odds and ends
//
// Purpose:	Demonstrates a simple AXI-Lite interface.
//
//	This was written in light of my last demonstrator, for which others
//	declared that it was much too complicated to understand.  The goal of
//	this demonstrator is to have logic that's easier to understand, use,
//	and copy as needed.
//
//	Since there are two basic approaches to AXI-lite signaling, both with
//	and without skidbuffers, this example demonstrates both so that the
//	differences can be compared and contrasted.
//
// Creator:	Dan Gisselquist, Ph.D.
//		Gisselquist Technology, LLC
//
